          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"undset-kristin-lavransdatter-efa06c87611829a4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Kristin directly avows that her love for Erlend had not diminished despite his earlier dishonourable treatment of her. The negation belongs to the comparative phrase “ikke mindre” (“no less”), so this is a strongly truth-conditional affirmation of continuing love, not a denial. It explains and reassures but does not undertake a commitment, and its careful explanatory framing supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Kristin’s original spoken avowal to Erlend is presented in direct quotation within her retrospective recollection of their earlier conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Erlend believes Kristin has secretly resented him over her pregnancy. Wanting him to understand otherwise, she recalls that she also bore no grudge when he previously treated her dishonourably and that she loved him no less. The utterance therefore functions principally as a deliberate retrospective avowal and rebuttal of his interpretation, with a secondary reassuring effect but no relational undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“det vet Gud og Maria mø, at jeg bar ikke nag, og jeg elsket dig ikke mindre” (“God and the Virgin Mary know that I bore no grudge, and I loved you no less”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit comparative negation affirms that Kristin’s love remained undiminished, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Men hun vilde saa gjerne at han skulde skjønne” (“But she wanted so much for him to understand”).\",\"supports\":\"Her words are presented as a deliberate explanation intended to correct Erlend’s belief, supporting assertive/avowal force rather than reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Erlend accuses Kristin of carrying “et lønlig nag” (a secret grudge), after which she contrasts the present dispute with his earlier dishonourable conduct toward her.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate conversational function is to deny resentment and explain the constancy of her love; reassurance in this setting does not by itself establish P.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The membership review marks the occurrence KEEP with confidence 0.99 as “VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU”; polarity is recorded as “surface_optional_negative.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the surface negation does not prevent the phrase from functioning as an explicit love avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal of undiminished love. The explanatory and reassuring context can be represented pragmatically without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The past tense concerns the time whose constancy is being asserted; it does not weaken the utterance’s truth-conditional avowal force.\"}"
        }
